106:All asynchronous context requests must be awaited. Pending requests are observed, drained and rejected at lifecycle boundaries; they cannot silently finish after output acceptance. Contexts close after their owner returns. Rolling back a transaction immediately closes every descendant context, so pending work cannot publish late analysis or fact records. Every asynchronous query captures artifact revisions, parameters, certificates and correspondences before awaiting caller code. Fact-set/explanation introspection can observe absent information, so its computations and dependants conservatively disable caching rather than pretending an incomplete dependency list captures absence.
108:## Evidence and transfer
110:Every property fact identifies its exact artifact ID, revision, kind and schema version, the property and checker versions, the supporting analysis, dependencies, current validity and assurance:
119:A correspondence contains `id`, exact `from` and `to` artifact references, `relation`, optional entity groups and application evidence. An IR definition may provide `entities(value)` to enumerate its entity IDs; when it does, correspondence checking compares the corresponding `sourceEntities` or `targetEntities` list against that artifact's IDs. `semanticCorrespondence` carries these lists from a generic IR correspondence. Transfer checks validate exact revisions and their semantic justification. Same-artifact preservation reuses the existing analysis without introducing a self-dependency. Source invalidation also invalidates transferred results.
121:## Transaction and compiler integration
128:const tx = await manager.beginTransformation(contract, namedInputs, {
141:Failed provisional analysis/fact records remain in history with invalidation explaining that they were discarded. They are not exposed as current facts and cannot authorize reuse. The execution still contains the rejected checks, exact revisions, effects and error, so failure is inspectable rather than disappearing.
143:### Computational reuse versus guarantee checks
145:A cached artifact is still checked under the current contract. The body has its own tracked dependency frame; pre/post checks do not masquerade as computational dependencies.
149:The v5 compiler adapter uses its generic pass policy hooks for this sequence. New artifacts are checked before the artifact store and computational cache accept them; cache hits are gated too. A rejected current guarantee cannot expose an old cached artifact as an accepted result of the new run.
151:## Revisioned effects
155:Resource access is checked transitively. An analysis's declared read does not grant its caller permission; the enclosing transformation must declare the effect too. Cached analysis reuse checks the same permissions and dependency revisions. Missing-resource reads record a `missing` revision before throwing, so a caught fallback is invalidated if the resource later appears.
159:These rules enforce use of the explicit effect port. They do not sandbox arbitrary JavaScript: an application implementation that directly changes global variables, files or network state outside the port violates the host contract and cannot be made pure by declaration. `cache:'pure'` is not a security boundary. The same qualification applies to caller-supplied validators and transfer/certificate verifiers; their versions and behavior remain an application responsibility.
161:## Scope
163:This module implements semantic pass contracts and analysis management. It does not introduce a universal AST, generic rewrite matcher, transaction-based IR editor, e-graph, static effect inference, persistent cache backend or formal proof engine. Those are separate architectural layers.
