23:## Transaction lifecycle
46:Each edit transaction is atomic. A sequence of several committed edits is not automatically one atomic transaction. The rewrite scheduler keeps earlier accepted revisions if a later candidate fails. An enclosing compiler pass has its own publication boundary.
48:## Differences and correspondence
56:Correspondence records association, not semantic equivalence. A domain checker must justify any preservation or analysis transfer that uses it.
58:## Patterns and rewriting
60:See [Patterns and rewrite schedules](PATTERNS-AND-REWRITES.md) for all builders, matching evidence, registries, seven schedules, order selection, budgets, scoped edit authority and failure statuses. Pure pattern/guard callbacks receive a frozen `IRReader`; rewrite callbacks receive a mutation-only `RewriteEdit`. The scheduler retains prepare/commit authority and seals callback access before semantic acceptance.
62:## Extension and trust boundaries
64:The package imposes a common logical inspection/edit protocol, not a physical storage model or universal semantic type system. Adapter callbacks, registered predicates, rewrite implementations and semantic verifiers are trusted TypeScript code. Read-only/mutation-only facades constrain the capabilities passed to callbacks; they are not a JavaScript sandbox against code holding unrelated references.
66:The revision store is in memory. Traces can be exported, but process recovery, distributed transactions, graph indexes, unbounded streaming and persistent cache services are separate concerns. There is no e-graph engine, SSA package, formal prover or automatic derivation of domain invariants.
