<div class="eyebrow part">Part two · the compiler, stage by stage</div>
<section id="t1">
  <div class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><h2>Stage 1: the Tix language, IR, evaluators and checked passes</h2><span class="tag ok">merged · IRWB-020-B · Astra</span></div>
    <p>Tix has five constructs: shaped parameters, pointwise expressions, <code>sum</code> and <code>max</code> reductions over named indices, single-assignment statements, and affine index arithmetic. Shapes are inferred and named (<code>N</code>, <code>D</code>), and indices may be affine (<code>x[i + dy, j + dx]</code>). Its f32 meaning is fixed down to the order of accumulation: every literal and operation is rounded to f32, and a reduction starts at 0 or −∞ and adds in increasing lexicographic order of its indices. Implementations can therefore be compared bit for bit.</p>
<pre class="code">fn layernorm(x: f32[N,D], gamma: f32[D], beta: f32[D], eps: f32[1]): f32[N,D] {
  mean[i] = (sum(j) x[i,j]) / D;
  centered[i,j] = x[i,j] - mean[i];
  variance[i] = (sum(j) centered[i,j] * centered[i,j]) / D;

