## Decision

Make a **validated transformation between named artifact ports** the unit that a strategy can select, cache or publish. Let a domain produce a candidate in its natural representation. Give the candidate a revision-scoped explanation of its edits and give the validator an explicit statement of what must hold. This is a change to who owns acceptance. It does not require rewriting either compiler into a common instruction set.

The motivating experiment takes one real Tix reduction split. Its exact gate rejected it. The stock strategy bridge ran the very same pass, returned a changed artifact and recorded no contract. Supplying a managed correspondence requirement revealed a second problem. The generic edit named the function and parameter as well as its statements, and the contract rejected that universe. Fixing only the async call site would leave both the semantic obligation and the vocabulary of the mapping unresolved. See "'`src/tensor/index.ts:39`, `src/framework/passes/bridge.ts:39`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:348`, `scripts/01-pass-boundary.mjs:17` and `scripts/01-pass-boundary.mjs:45`.

**Evidence conventions.** Unqualified source paths refer to review base `93ec5fc`. Ticket-relative paths begin with `scripts/` or `various/`. Prefixes such as **T6-D**, **A0-G** and **B1-F** identify the corresponding sibling and revision in the [31-cell survey](../various/cell-survey.md). The table distinguishes the committed diff from working-copy source and gives each cell'"'s own record. Its counts cover changed paths under src and tools relative to t6-base, not completed deliverables. D1-G is also the merged D1 exemplar, so it supplies one design lineage. Runtime conclusions below identify the probe. A branch source citation alone describes an implementation choice. GPU claims in the historical reports are the authors' measurements. This review ran CPU probes.

## What the comparison changes

### A pass is currently too small to carry its own experiment

The pass spec couples its input IR, adapter, write vocabulary, needs and optional contract to one type S. A native result returns another S. The managed transformation contract already speaks named input and output ports. The CUDA lowering registers baseline, function and fusion option as inputs and CUDA as an output. It hand-assembles the kernel correspondence and calls the planner and emitter inside its body. The direct source and execution APIs separately assemble the lowering. These are three different responsibilities: constructing a candidate, choosing how to lower, and deciding whether the candidate may enter a checked experiment. "'`src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:70`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:79`, `src/framework/contracts/types.ts:137`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:83`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:203`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:221`, `src/tensor/index.ts:66`.

The synchronous native runner prohibits needs, preservation and managed contracts. It requires a host gate on changed candidates. The managed runner resolves needs, supports native results with a lazy edit and can confirm a preservation claim by recomputation. Its gate is optional. The meaningful asymmetry is the *ownership and availability of evidence*. Native computation itself works inside a strategy. The probe deliberately requests exactness for split, which the public Tix optimizer ordinarily checks under tolerance. It compares the same requested relation on the two outputs; it does not diagnose an error in the public optimizer'"'s choice of relation. "'`src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:231`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:250`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:276`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:302`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:373`, `src/tensor/index.ts:42`, `scripts/01-pass-boundary.mjs:15`.

The T6 alternatives form an experiment in API placement. E implements five strategy cases in executeSync; H implements a separate sequence/apply evaluator. J registers fused, tiled and pipelined steps with an explicit gate in the bridge. D adds a synchronous legality predicate consulted by both runners before the body. The D predicate receives before/view/params, so an implementation needing a fact has no declared fact port there. Preserve the distinction between an inapplicable candidate and an incorrect candidate. Carry those outcomes through the existing strategy operators. The J registration is the smallest migration experiment; the D precondition motivates a richer operation signature. **T6-E** `src/framework/strategy/sync.ts:37`; **T6-H** `src/framework/strategy/sync.ts:94`; **T6-J** `src/tensor/schedule-plan.ts:33`; **T6-D** `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:101`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:264`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:305`; merged `src/framework/strategy/interpreter.ts:487`, `src/framework/passes/bridge.ts:57`.

### The entity universe is part of the type of a correspondence

Tix projects function, parameter and statement entities. Each statement'"'s entire expression is an attribute. Its read relation uses a set of names, so two reads at different index expressions become one dependency edge. Its contract enumerates only statement IDs. The native inline pass returns a module without explicit mappings. On a permuted producer/consumer pair the generic diff retained f/y and removed f/p. It knew which tensor disappeared, but supplied no edge from the inlined producer to the surviving consumer. The lowering is finer: its mapping groups the contributing statement IDs for each kernel. "'`src/tensor/ir.ts:100`, `src/tensor/ir.ts:105`, `src/tensor/ir.ts:125`, `src/tensor/passes.ts:89`, `src/framework/ir/correspondence.ts:116`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:95`, `scripts/01-pass-boundary.mjs:27`.

F23 and F24 solve real identity problems. The manager checks the enumerated ID sets against the supplied lists, then checks membership of entries. The generic IR validator additionally requires every endpoint to occur in an entry. A managed transformation requiring a correspondence accepted **zero groups** with both correct lists in the probe. Conversely it rejected a complete generic edit using the adapter'"'s broader universe. A full “statement to selected function's kernels” contract also needs an explicit policy for other functions' statements. Requiring blanket totality across the entire module would make valid function selection look like deletion. "'`BACKLOG.md:483`, `src/framework/contracts/analysis-manager.ts:1110`, `src/framework/ir/correspondence.ts:85`, `src/framework/ir/correspondence.ts:158`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:93`, `scripts/01-pass-boundary.mjs:45`.

The same missing information costs Typed work twice. A0-G reconstructs chains of replacements, ownership of new locals and control-flow shapes. A0-C reimplements matching, guards and instantiation in its census tool. Typed'"'s rule compiler already has the matched rule and bindings *before* it instantiates the new tree. D1-G joins machine counts to the first covered statement, assigns residual costs to frame/backend and joins new sites through the first origin. D1-D constructs lineage across steps. The census asks “what edit occurred?” and attribution asks “where was work observed?” A change ledger can supply shared input to both, while keeping their measures distinct. "'`src/typed/rules/compile.ts:124`, `src/typed/tx.ts:47`, `src/typed/passes.ts:128`, `src/typed/attribute.ts:89`, `src/typed/attribute.ts:105`; **A0-G** `src/typed/edit-classes.ts:283`, **A0-C** `tools/edit-census.mjs:21`, **D1-D** `src/typed/attribution.ts:88`. For the other independent implementations see `various/cell-survey.md:13` through `various/cell-survey.md:26`.

The disagreement is measurable even without benchmarking another compiler. Across the same 2,021 O2 edit records, A0-B reports 203 local edits and G reports 395. Both recover 183 verified-rule matches. G includes rewrites justified by input facts among locals; B separates a narrower local arithmetic class. Those are reported census results, not rerun totals. A framework enum called “local” would standardize an ambiguity. Record the match, footprint and premises separately so both classifications can be computed by query. **A0-B** `ttmp/2026/11/27/IRWB-035-B--local-value-rewrites-in-o2-and-speed/analysis/01-corpus-results.md:29` and `:38`; **A0-G** `ttmp/2026/11/27/IRWB-035-G--what-share-of-the-optimizer-s-edits-are-local-value-rewrites/reference/02-report-the-share-of-local-value-rewrites-among-the-optimizer-s-edits.md:33` and `:66`.

D1 also shows when *domain analysis*, rather than more infrastructure, is the right fix. A loop header can have several latches. The merged natural-loop computation groups dominance backedges by header and takes predecessor closure starting from all its latches, then finds exterior predecessors. The regression fixture checks the two-latch case and verifies that attribution rows sum to each emulator delta. Keep the loop definition with the CFG analyses and let a correspondence consumer account for cost. Neither an exact accounting identity nor a site'"'s first origin proves which edit caused a speed change in isolation. "'`src/typed/analyses.ts:215`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:227`, `src/typed/attribute.ts:185`, `tests/typed/attribute.test.mjs:43`, `tests/typed/attribute.test.mjs:58`, `scripts/03-merged-fixtures.sh:4`.

### Binding time is two problems with different termination conditions

B1-G and F implement an offline division inside Typed: a value is static if the bound inputs determine it, subject to permitted operations and memory reads. G starts with candidate static locals and removes those with dynamic assignments until stable. E explicitly calls its online trace the analysis. B keys residual block versions by original block and live static environment; A also maintains variants and refuses stores that may alias a promised buffer. F budgets emitted statements, G budgets variants relative to bound cells. D has working-copy binding analysis despite a docs/probe-only committed diff. C'"'s final report explicitly records a scalar-binding negative control and an unfinished constant-buffer specializer. Most cells implement binding-time reasoning locally. Only a subset actually needs a standalone offline analysis. **B1-G** "'`src/typed/bta.ts:65`, `src/typed/bta.ts:91`, `src/typed/specialize-buffer.ts:216`; **B1-F** `src/typed/pe.ts:76`, `src/typed/pe.ts:271`; **B1-E** `src/typed/peval.ts:32`, **B1-B** `src/typed/residualize.ts:61`, **B1-A** `src/typed/specialize-memory.ts:57`, `src/typed/specialize-memory.ts:154`; `various/cell-survey.md:27` through `various/cell-survey.md:33`.

I represented unknown dependencies by a set lattice, used variables as nodes, and compared the merged solver with the actual G division on cyclic copies. Both found only local 3 static. The worklist took six visits, round-robin ten. This establishes that an ordinary finite taint problem already fits the solver. The harder missing service interns and schedules *new residual program points* as they are discovered. A static CFG solver whose block set is fixed at entry cannot express that operation just by changing lattice. A new entry block is also a semantic edit; G manually moves it into position after constructing it. `scripts/02-binding-times.mjs:13`, `scripts/02-binding-times.mjs:36`, `src/framework/dataflow/solver.ts:17`, `src/framework/dataflow/solver.ts:100`; **B1-G** `src/typed/specialize-buffer.ts:290`; `BACKLOG.md:490`.

### A schedule needs an independent statement of legality

The merged planner fuses adjacent producers and consumers by common axis prefix and access form, then decides thread/block mapping, row-buffer reuse, and whether staging is worthwhile. Its cost thresholds and string-valued launch predicate sit beside correctness decisions. Even the runtime recovers the gated kernel names by matching generated CUDA text. A schedule IR should own that information through emission and execution. Retaining a source string as a terminal artifact is fine; recovering the experiment'"'s arm labels from the pretty-printer is avoidable. "'`src/tensor/fusion.ts:83`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:107`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:135`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:142`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:229`, `src/tensor/cuda-source.ts:148`, `src/tensor/cuda-runtime.ts:142`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:187`.

T6-B seeds a schedule from planFusion; G consults it in a scheduling pass; A calls it on fragments; E recomputes it after isolation; C even compares the candidate with another invocation of the planner. C'"'s own interim report describes a foundation with decisions still in the emitter. H converts a fusion plan, I seeds passes from one and J also invokes it on the schedule's program. D goes further by giving split, reorder, bind and stage separate operations, and E evaluates the candidate schedule on samples. Reusing a planner as a *candidate producer* is sensible. Using agreement with it as the only specification would reject a better, legal schedule and accept a shared planner error. **T6-B** "'`src/tensor/schedule.ts:504`, **T6-G** `src/tensor/schedule-passes.ts:103`, **T6-A** `src/tensor/schedule.ts:268`, **T6-E** `src/tensor/schedule-passes.ts:49`, `src/tensor/schedule-eval.ts:399`, **T6-C** `src/tensor/schedule.ts:61`, **T6-H** `src/tensor/schedule.ts:254`, **T6-D** `src/tensor/schedule.ts:539`, `src/tensor/schedule.ts:633`, `various/cell-survey.md:3` through `various/cell-survey.md:12`.

