## Alternatives and rejection criteria

**Adopt a universal editable graph.** I would decline. A logical read projection is already sufficient for matching, diff and provenance. A Tix tensor carries expression trees and bound indices inside a statement; a graph of tensor names cannot reconstruct capture-avoiding substitutions. Typed's Tx has ordering and containment operations, while B1-G's entry edit exposes a specific missing operation. Extend edit capabilities where a real producer can supply their semantics. Try a read-only expression/axis projection and a native replacement at a focus before designing generic binder edits. Keep the existing generic rewrite schedules for domains with an editable projection. "'`src/tensor/ir.ts:105`, `src/tensor/passes.ts:12`, `src/framework/ir/types.ts:76`, `src/framework/ir/PATTERNS-AND-REWRITES.md:111`, **B1-G** `src/typed/specialize-buffer.ts:290`.

**Give each latency mode its own strategy language.** I would decline. The E and H subsets have different operators and traces. A host can require immediate execution for a known fragment, or expose an asynchronous general plan. Define one transition semantics for branch isolation, recovery, budget accounting and validation. Start with an async plan calling the existing synchronous kernel plus its explicit gate. Implement a synchronous plan evaluator only if real synchronous clients need *plan-level branching* and parity can be tested using the same instructions and outcome schema. **T6-E** `src/framework/strategy/sync.ts:43`, **T6-H** `src/framework/strategy/sync.ts:107`, **T6-J** `src/tensor/schedule-plan.ts:33`, `src/framework/strategy/README.md:80`.

**Use pass legality as the semantic checker.** I would decline. An input precondition establishes that a rule *may* apply; an output checker can audit independent candidate producers and cached outputs. Both should be able to name the same established fact, yet neither should call the same planner as its only oracle. The T6-D guard caught the permutation its checker missed. C'"'s planner equality is useful as a regression oracle for one implementation. Neither supplies a correctness condition for arbitrary new schedules. **T6-D** "'`src/tensor/schedule.ts:589`, **T6-C** `src/tensor/schedule.ts:61`, `scripts/05-reduction-order.mjs:26`.

**Replace every lowering check with local proofs.** I would decline. An integer rewrite can have a checkable algebraic derivation. Its application still needs a valid substitution, discharged side conditions, coverage of all edits and a semantics-preserving context. The A0-G probe recovered mul-one and add-zero, validated the result inside a subtraction, and refused an unexplained edit to the sibling operand. Device compilation, host buffer layout, and execution profile remain distinct obligations even if a symbolic schedule check succeeds. Keep the end-to-end reference check and name its scope. `scripts/06-rewrite-scope.mjs:13`, `src/typed/rules/verify.ts:63`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:169`, `src/tensor/cuda-runtime.ts:138`.

**Promote empirical thresholds into universal cost facts.** I would decline. Keep the shape predicate, selected arm and observation protocol as reproducible data. A prediction may propose a choice. A measured choice is evidence on the measured shapes and hardware profile. Tie the winner to that comparison and retain the fallback arm and uncertainty. This also applies to Typed speed versus O2. Add a new cost-analysis package only after both clients agree on an observation protocol and on which quantities are comparable. `src/tensor/fusion.ts:107`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:135`, `src/typed/strategies.ts:66`, `src/typed/strategies.ts:175`, `BACKLOG.md:485`.

